    "q-175902": {
      "dom_id": "step-q175902",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "If $p=-2$ and $q=6,$ then $\\frac{2q-3}{4}+\\frac{4}{4-2p}=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Replacing $p$ with $-2$ and $q$ with $6$ in the expression, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2q-3}{4}+\\frac{4}{4-2p} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(6)-3}{4}+\\frac{4}{4-2(-2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{12-3}{4}+\\frac{4}{4+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{4}{8} & = \\\\ \\frac{18}{8}+\\frac{4}{8} & = \\\\ \\frac{18+4}{8} & = \\\\ \\frac{22}{8} & = \\\\ \\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
